Pass the edict to gi.cprintf, not to a variadic format argument

Applies to q2admin at commit 15b7a7b5f727aa3de303ab9660d4f195aea4f506.
`server/enginepatch.sh` checks that it still does.

Carried rather than forked: upstream `packetflinger/q2admin` is the build's
source and stays that way, because one line is not a fork's worth of divergence
-- and a fork pinned for one line is a fork somebody has to remember to unpin.
This project ran on `Niehztog/q2admin` until it did, and the pin went stale
within days: the branch was rebased, the pinned SHA stopped being reachable
from any branch, and `git clone` + `git checkout --detach` -- which is exactly
what the Dockerfile does -- failed with "reference is not a tree".

WHAT IT FIXES

`q2a_printf` is `void q2a_printf(char *fmt, ...)` (`g_util.h:34`): a console
printer whose first argument is the format string.  `c78b84c "Added q2a_printf
for console output"` swapped a `gi.cprintf` call in `reloadWhoisFileRun` for it
WITHOUT dropping the two arguments that belonged to the old signature, so the
`edict_t *` is passed as the format and `PRINT_HIGH` and the real message
follow it as varargs:

    q2a_printf(ent, PRINT_HIGH, "whois file reloaded.\n");

The other twenty-one `q2a_printf` calls in the tree are correct; this is the
only site, and it is the only one that had an `ent` to get wrong.

TWO CONSEQUENCES, and the build hits the first one.

  * `-Wincompatible-pointer-types` is an ERROR from GCC 14, so Debian trixie
    refuses the file outright -- `g_whois.c:324:16: error`, Makefile:206,
    exit 2.  Confirmed by building upstream tip here before writing this.
  * On an older compiler it is a warning, and the call reads an `edict_t` as a
    format string: undefined behaviour waiting for somebody to type the
    command.

WHY gi.cprintf AND NOT A CORRECTED q2a_printf

The message is addressed to whoever typed the command, which is what the `ent`
was for; `c78b84c` replaced `gi.cprintf(ent, PRINT_HIGH, "Whois file
reloaded.\n")` and the console printer cannot say that.  `g_checkvar.c:171` is
the same sentence for the same kind of command and still spells it
`gi.cprintf(ent, PRINT_HIGH, "Check-Variables reloaded.\n")`.  So this restores
the call and keeps upstream's re-worded message, which makes it one token wide
and gives the next bump the smallest possible thing to conflict with.

diff --git a/g_whois.c b/g_whois.c
index e364c13..03ab9e2 100644
--- a/g_whois.c
+++ b/g_whois.c
@@ -321,5 +321,5 @@ void whois_read_file(void) {
  */
 void reloadWhoisFileRun(int startarg, edict_t *ent, int client) {
     whois_read_file();
-    q2a_printf(ent, PRINT_HIGH, "whois file reloaded.\n");
+    gi.cprintf(ent, PRINT_HIGH, "whois file reloaded.\n");
 }
